        "note_tweet": {
          "entities": {},
          "text": "If we must, must implement age verification, I present a framework that I think most people would at least tolerate:\n\n- Keep the current EU ZKP framework. Its base ideas are good. The problem is the private signing key cannot be extracted, otherwise the system is broken (tldr you can sign infinitely many proofs and share them online)\n- Instead of an app, manufacture Yubikey-like hardware keys with a private key stored within, in non-exportable memory.\n- Send those to town halls.\n\nNow, an adult:\n- Goes to a town hall.\n- Shows ID.\n- Puts their hand into a box with 20 of these and randomly picks one.\n- The adult says \"thank you\", smiles and leaves.\n\nNow:\n- During first time use, the key asks to set up a PIN\n- Once that is done, verification is done by simply plugging the device in (or NFC), inputting the PIN, and touching a button on the device.\n\nPros:\n- EU's ZKP framework guarantees anonymous and non-linkable proofs - good!\n- Hardware key imposes no Software / OS / etc limits - good!\n- Hardware key can enforce basic rate limits - good!\n\nI welcome discussion"
        },
